Report a date -r file time that localtime() can't convert.
The Microsoft C runtime's localtime() returns NULL for a time before
1970 or after the year 3000, and the localtime_r() wrapper substitutes
the epoch for such a time. So date -r on a file with a modification time
out of that range printed 1970-01-01 00:00:00 UTC, with the file's own
nanoseconds, and exited with status 0.

Check the time with localtime_or_null() and report it as out of range,
with status 1, as GNU date does.

---
 coreutils/date.c |    6 ++++++
 1 file changed, 6 insertions(+)

--- a/coreutils/date.c
+++ b/coreutils/date.c
@@ -248,6 +248,12 @@
 		struct stat statbuf;
 		xstat(filename, &statbuf);
 		ts.tv_sec = statbuf.st_mtime;
+#if ENABLE_PLATFORM_MINGW32
+		/* localtime_r() would give the epoch for an invalid time */
+		if (localtime_or_null(&ts.tv_sec) == NULL)
+			bb_error_msg_and_die("time '%"LL_FMT"d' is out of range",
+					(long long)ts.tv_sec);
+#endif
 #if ENABLE_FEATURE_DATE_NANO
 		ts.tv_nsec = statbuf.st_mtim.tv_nsec;
 		/* Some toolchains use .st_mtimensec instead of st_mtim.tv_nsec.
